# A public URL for Superset, Dex and dex-register (T40, ADR 0035), through a Cloudflare
# Tunnel you already run on this machine, for another project -- reusing it instead of a
# second tunnel or opening a port on your router. Optional, personal, never committed:
#
#   cp docker-compose.override.yml.dist docker-compose.override.yml
#
# Compose reads that file automatically next to docker-compose.yml, alongside
# docker-compose.yml itself (no flag needed); .gitignore keeps the real one out.
#
# 1. Find the Docker network your `cloudflared` container is already on:
#      docker inspect <container> --format '{{.HostConfig.NetworkMode}}'
#    and put it in `.env` as PFP_TUNNEL_NETWORK=that-network-name.
# 2. In Cloudflare Zero Trust (Networks > Tunnels > your tunnel > Public Hostname), add
#    one route per service, to the *container* name and port below (not 127.0.0.1: once
#    these services share a network with `cloudflared`, it reaches them by that name) --
#      superset.yourdomain.com  -> http://superset:8088
#      dex.yourdomain.com       -> http://dex:5556
#      signup.yourdomain.com    -> http://dex-register:5559
#    That last route, and only it, is meant to be shared outside your household: it is
#    the one page a stranger with the link but not the invite code can reach and get
#    nothing from.
#
# Every service keeps its 127.0.0.1 port too (bi/docker-compose.yml): this only adds a
# second, public way in, it does not remove the local one.

networks:
  tunnel:
    name: ${PFP_TUNNEL_NETWORK}
    external: true

services:
  superset:
    networks: [default, tunnel]
  dex:
    networks: [default, tunnel]
  dex-register:
    networks: [default, tunnel]
